WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.715 --> 00:00:01.890
- This episode of LegalEagle

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.603
was made possible by Skillshare.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.470
Believe it or not, I
actually was not going to do

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:08.320
another election lawsuit video,

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:09.950
but just when I think I'm out,

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:11.620
they pull me back in.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:14.050
- Just when I thought I was out,

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:15.600
they pull me back in.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:16.720
- And here, (laughs)

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.610
there's actually so much
hilarious stuff that's going on

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:20.690
that you'd never know it

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:22.850
if you've never litigated
in federal court before.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:23.747
I was going to say,

00:00:23.747 --> 00:00:25.470
"You wouldn't know it
if you hadn't practiced

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.160
at a high level in federal court before,"

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.920
but honestly, even if you've practiced

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.260
at a low level in federal court,

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.830
you'd know how crazy
these shenanigans are.

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:35.270
Because, frankly, when
democracy is at stake,

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.060
the only thing that you can do is laugh,

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:40.830
but I feel like it's incumbent
upon me to share the humor,

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.930
to explain why some of the
things that are happening

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:44.750
in these Trump campaign lawsuits

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.730
are so particularly funny.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:50.580
Why the Trump lawyers
are so, so bad at this

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:53.800
and why they keep failing
in spectacular fashion.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.400
Because so far, they've
been walking into rakes,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.473
and they will continue to walk into rakes.

00:00:59.473 --> 00:01:02.140
(stately music)

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.660
And at base, these lawsuits
are just going down in flames.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:08.460
They're either being dismissed
by the Trump campaign itself

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.610
and not wanting to prosecute
or litigate these cases,

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:13.230
or they're getting
dismissed by the judges.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:14.800
And recently we have

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:16.630
some momentous decisions in Pennsylvania

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:19.550
that seem to be a bellwether
for the rest of these cases,

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:22.100
and other states are
just simply certifying

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.300
the election results
regardless of the lawsuits

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.910
and it doesn't seem like these lawsuits

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:27.870
are going to change the results.

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:30.220
And just because the lawsuits themselves

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:31.070
don't have any merit

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:32.900
doesn't mean that they
can't also screw it up

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:34.640
by litigating them poorly.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.700
Now, today we're going to
update some of the lawsuits

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.030
that we previously talked about,

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:39.840
talk about some of the new
lawsuits that have been filed

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.240
and a lot of the oral
argument that has taken place,

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.970
and various filings that have been filed

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:45.390
with the federal courts.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:47.760
But first, I'm afraid we have to start

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.140
with the insane press conference
that happened at the RNC

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:54.190
that was led by Rudy
Giuliani and Sidney Powell,

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:56.930
two of President Trump's
remaining lawyers.

00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:59.590
Now, this was one of the
wildest press conferences

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:01.920
ever given on behalf of the
president of the United States,

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.450
and that includes the one given at

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:06.620
the Four Seasons Total Landscaping.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:09.100
Now, you might have seen clips
from this press conference,

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:10.750
not only because President
Trump tweeted out

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:13.720
that this was going to
reveal a path to victory,

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:16.890
but also because Rudy Giuliani
had a meltdown on camera,

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:18.610
and I'm not saying that metaphorically.

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:21.140
He appeared to literally melt down.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.640
We're not exactly sure what
happened to Rudy Giuliani,

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.820
but it appears that profuse sweating

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:27.460
mixed with some hair dye

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:29.960
led to rivulets of black
liquid pouring down

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:31.810
both sides of his face.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:34.570
Now, people are wondering
why Rudy Giuliani

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:38.430
would even bother to put hair
dye on his remaining hair,

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.760
but I think this sort of
makes sense in a weird way.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.760
There's a theory of
neurolinguistics that says

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.930
that if you adopt the vocal mannerisms

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:47.380
of a person that you're talking to

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:48.820
and their physical characteristics,

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:50.600
then they're more likely
to agree with you.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.260
So if you are the lawyer for the man

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:56.410
who has the most famous
bad hair job in history,

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:58.960
then it sorta makes sense
that you would do crazy things

00:02:58.960 --> 00:02:59.900
to your own hair.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:02.620
It's really just good client management.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:05.797
And here is Rudy talking
about and reenacting

00:03:05.797 --> 00:03:07.620
"My Cousin Vinny."

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.100
- Did you all watch "My Cousin Vinny"?

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:10.933
Do you know the movie?

00:03:10.933 --> 00:03:13.310
It's one of my favorite law movies

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:14.940
'cause he comes from Brooklyn,

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.670
and when the nice lady who said she saw,

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:20.493
and then he says to her,

00:03:21.657 --> 00:03:23.207
"How many fingers do I got up?"

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.507
And she says, "Three."

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:31.030
Well, she was too far away
to see it was only two.

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:33.030
These people were further away

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:35.440
than "My Cousin Vinny"
was from the witness.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:36.810
- Which, good for Rudy.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:40.000
He does more or less
accurately recount that scene

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.570
in "My Cousin Vinny,"

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:43.840
but it's hard not to think of Andy Dwyer

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:45.590
recounting his favorite movies

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:48.370
when you hear Rudy Giuliani
off on this weird tangent

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:49.540
about this particular movie.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:51.300
Yes, that's a long way of saying

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:54.510
that things that are further
away are harder to see,

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:57.430
and there are reports that
Giuliani was enticed to come back

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:00.860
as the president's lawyer by
being paid $20,000 per day,

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:02.010
so I would just like to say

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:04.690
that if anyone wants to
pay me $20,000 per day,

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:07.550
I also know "My Cousin
Vinny" very, very well

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:10.350
and I can recite scenes
from "My Cousin Vinny"

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:13.140
with at least the kind of
fidelity that Rudy Giuliani has.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.920
- Eggs and grits. I like grits too.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:17.700
- Now, I think it's
interesting to point out

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.480
that at this point in the litigation,

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:20.980
President Trump is not represented

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.790
by the lawyers that represented him

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:23.920
in the impeachment hearings.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:27.210
People like Jay Sekulow,
John Dowd and Ty Cobb.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:29.810
Now, say what you will about
those particular lawyers,

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:31.060
they have an active practice

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:33.270
with a whole fleet of
lawyers that work for them

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.130
and they have certainly gotten results

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:36.580
in different contexts,

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:38.170
but Rudy Giuliani, it appears,

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:42.160
hasn't appeared in
federal court since 1992.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:43.720
But it's not just Rudy Giuliani.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.370
Trump campaign lawyer, Sidney Powell,

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:48.200
blamed Venezuela, Cuba, and likely China

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:49.470
for Trump's losses.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:52.910
- Massive influence of communist money.

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:55.460
- Powell used to be a
reasonably respected lawyer,

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:56.620
but recently she's gone

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:58.950
full deep state conspiracy tinfoil hat,

00:04:58.950 --> 00:04:59.960
and not for nothing,

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:01.910
she is also the lawyer for Mike Flynn.

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:03.440
Take that as you will.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:04.720
But Sidney Powell's theory is

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.360
that the Dominion voting
machines used throughout America

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.700
were originally engineered by Hugo Chavez

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:11.910
to ensure that he never lost an election,

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:14.820
despite the fact that Chavez
has been dead since 2013,

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:17.330
and hand recounts have confirmed

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:19.670
that Biden beat Trump in various states,

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:22.170
and it would be easy to
check the electronic records

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:23.810
against the physical ballots,

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:25.250
but don't let that get in the way

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:26.840
of a great conspiracy theory.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:30.130
Of course, Rudy also dismissed
the hand recount out of hand

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:32.270
by reenacting how the
votes could have been cast

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:33.900
for Mickey Mouse or a dead person

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:35.670
or one person voting 30 times.

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:37.420
- That could have been Mickey Mouse.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.400
- All these examples
really happened, he says,

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:41.600
bereft of any evidence whatsoever.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:42.830
And Jenna Ellis,

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:47.330
the third lawyer for the Trump
campaign in this conference,

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:49.450
explained that people asking
for evidence of these claims

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:51.520
just don't understand how
the legal process works.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:53.580
- Your question is fundamentally flawed

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.120
when you're asking,
"Where's the evidence?"

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:57.400
What we have asked for in the court

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:02.400
is to not have the
certification of false results.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:04.110
- And, yeah, fair enough.

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:05.540
If you call a press conference,

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:07.860
you are not obligated
to provide the evidence,

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:11.010
but this press conference is
not for the court's benefit

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:12.040
but for the public's benefit

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:13.180
and if you have the evidence

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:15.600
you might actually want
to present that evidence,

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.410
both in the court of public
opinion and in the actual court,

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:20.040
but we've seen time and time again

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:22.590
that the Trump campaign is
just simply not reiterating

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:25.400
these grand conspiracy theories in court

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:27.620
where they have the
potential to be sanctioned.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:29.840
And Sidney Powell went on
to claim without evidence

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:30.673
that in the middle of the night,

00:06:30.673 --> 00:06:31.830
someone from Dominion came in

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:34.220
and injected the votes into Detroit.

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:37.150
- Well, I'm talking about
some massive straight lines up

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:39.930
in the vote tallies in
the middle of the night

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:42.120
after they've supposedly stopped counting,

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:45.630
and that's when the
Dominion operators went in

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:48.360
and injected votes and
changed the whole system.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.210
- We've already seen
effectively zero discrepancies

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:53.730
where there's a hand count
of the Dominion machines.

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:54.690
And interestingly,

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:56.860
Sidney Powell, after this conference,

00:06:56.860 --> 00:06:59.290
went on to claim that
Georgia governor, Brian Kemp,

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:02.790
conspired with Democrats to
win Georgia for the presidency

00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:04.890
but not the Senate seats,

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:08.690
and perhaps as a result of these claims,

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:11.650
Sidney Powell was effectively
dismissed shortly thereafter

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:13.810
by Jenna Ellis via tweet,

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:17.050
which can only be described
as a public knifing.

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:20.260
It was pretty brutal to
just publicly disavow her

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:22.780
after weeks of saying
that Sidney Powell is

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:25.470
the tip of the spear of
this Trump campaign effort

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:27.280
and just being completely sidelined,

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:29.940
though Rudy Giuliani
has not been sidelined,

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:31.490
which is interesting.

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:33.020
This was a legal meltdown

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:34.930
for all of the Trump campaign lawyers,

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:36.050
not just Sidney Powell,

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:37.830
and especially Rudy Giuliani,

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:39.770
so it's interesting that he's staying on.

00:07:39.770 --> 00:07:42.970
And because everything is now a metaphor,

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:44.190
to add insult to injury,

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:47.360
or I suppose snot to hair dye,

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:48.470
there's a point in this conference

00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:51.070
where Rudy sneezes into a handkerchief,

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:52.120
flips the handkerchief over,

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:53.790
and then smears it all over his face

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:55.730
in an attempt to get the hair dye off.

00:07:55.730 --> 00:07:59.270
That pretty much sums up this
conference in a nutshell.

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:00.890
Although the Trump campaign legal team

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:03.579
has referred to themselves
as an elite strike force.

00:08:03.579 --> 00:08:06.330
- This is an elite strike force team.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:08.260
- As David Fahrenthold said,

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:11.210
they're more like the Fyre
Festival of the legal world.

00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:13.870
Which is a great segue into
the actual cases themselves,

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:16.650
and one of the cases in
particular in Pennsylvania

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:18.870
gave rise to one of the
most epic oral arguments

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:20.950
I've ever heard in my life,

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:23.650
as well as one of the
most interesting decisions

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:24.483
by the court,

00:08:24.483 --> 00:08:26.260
which we'll get to both in a second,

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:29.760
but first I want to say a debt
of gratitude to Marc Elias

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:32.280
and his team at democracydocket.com.

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:34.480
Not only is Marc and
his team keeping tabs on

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:35.960
all the different election suits,

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:38.280
but they're often in the front lines

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:39.920
litigating this stuff in court.

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:42.640
Democracy Docket has been a
godsend in trying to keep track

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:43.920
of all of these different cases,

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:45.480
and there are a lot of them,

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:48.450
so if you were to go to
democracydocket.com and donate,

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:49.900
I would definitely not blame you,

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:51.610
and I think I'll go there myself.

00:08:51.610 --> 00:08:54.080
But as Marc and his team
are want to point out,

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:55.820
at this point, the Trump campaign

00:08:55.820 --> 00:08:59.340
and the Trump administration
in court are about 2 for 35,

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:01.590
and the two that they've
won are barely wins.

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:03.310
They're more like participation trophies.

00:09:03.310 --> 00:09:06.270
So it's not to say that these lawsuits

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:07.510
should not have been brought

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:09.530
and that they're good for
democracy or anything,

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:13.210
but that is to say that they
are losing pretty spectacularly

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:15.360
and that ratio is unlikely to change

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:17.330
and they're unlikely to have any effect

00:09:17.330 --> 00:09:18.725
on the results of the election whatsoever.

00:09:18.725 --> 00:09:20.900
(screen chimes)

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:23.060
Hey, it is a future Devin,

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:25.130
and things have been moving so fast

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:27.780
that in the time it has taken
to put this video together,

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:29.360
things have actually changed dramatically.

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:30.370
So first of all,

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:33.640
the Trump campaign is more
like 1 and 39 at this point.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:36.130
They actually lost one of their victories,

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:37.670
so you can take one
away from the win column

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:39.740
and now it is in the loss column,

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:43.130
and there has been a very,
very interesting appeal

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:45.230
that has come out which I will talk about

00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:46.900
towards the end of the video.

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:49.340
So, back to prior Devin.

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:50.240
Wa!
(screen chimes)

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:53.620
But with that caveat, let's
get into the Pennsylvania case.

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:55.540
It's one of the more consequential cases,

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:56.880
and we've talked about it before,

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:58.310
which is Donald J. Trump for President

00:09:58.310 --> 00:09:59.920
versus Kelly Boockvar.

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:02.570
This started out as the case

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:05.770
that referred to pole
watchers, spelled P-O-L-E,

00:10:05.770 --> 00:10:08.680
which you would expect for
maybe a case in New Jersey,

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:10.110
but not Pennsylvania.

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:13.640
But this whole case is a lesson
in basic civil procedure.

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:17.050
I could probably teach an
entire class on civil procedure

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:19.750
just using the court's
decision in this case

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:22.580
that came out just a couple
of days ago over the weekend.

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:24.750
So quick summary of this case for context.

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:27.490
Here the Trump campaign, on
behalf of several voters,

00:10:27.490 --> 00:10:30.590
sued the Pennsylvania Secretary
of State, Kathy Boockvar.

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:32.500
They made two main claims,

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:34.020
one related to curing ballots

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:35.640
and the other related to poll watchers,

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:36.850
and on that basis,

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:38.160
effectively the Trump campaign sought

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:40.560
to de-certify the results in Pennsylvania

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:42.750
or prevent the certification
from happening.

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:44.480
Now, related to the curing of ballots,

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:46.810
they claim that these
two individual voters

00:10:46.810 --> 00:10:48.550
had their votes disregarded

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:50.580
and that different voters in the state,

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:51.940
based on what county you are,

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:55.550
were able to cure their
ballots if they were defective.

00:10:55.550 --> 00:10:57.620
Some counties adopted a process

00:10:57.620 --> 00:10:58.910
where you could cure the ballots

00:10:58.910 --> 00:11:00.350
and some counties is not,

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:02.270
and these individuals lived in a county

00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:04.730
where you couldn't cure
those particular ballots

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:07.110
because they were manifestly defective.

00:11:07.110 --> 00:11:09.940
And then the second was
related to poll watchers

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:11.370
not being close enough.

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:12.910
Now at this point we're
gonna have to get into

00:11:12.910 --> 00:11:14.700
some civil procedure, but trust me,

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:15.533
it will be worth it

00:11:15.533 --> 00:11:17.380
because it's really
incredibly interesting.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:19.230
Because originally in this case,

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:21.280
the Trump campaign issued a complaint,

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:22.990
which is the document
that starts the lawsuit.

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:24.570
It contains all of your
factual allegations.

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:26.430
The story of what you say happens.

00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:28.150
The original complaint contained

00:11:28.150 --> 00:11:30.930
all kinds of allegations of fraud,

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:33.660
but you're allowed,
within certain timeframes,

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:35.400
to amend your complaint,

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:37.270
and new lawyers came on,

00:11:37.270 --> 00:11:39.270
and effectively they
were adults in the room,

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:40.920
removed all the allegations of fraud,

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:42.600
for reasons we'll talk about in a second,

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:45.740
and then the Trump campaign
wanted to reverse that

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:47.930
and add the fraud allegations back.

00:11:47.930 --> 00:11:50.010
Now, the timeline goes
something like this.

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:51.960
This lawsuit was filed on November 9th

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:53.150
by lawyers at Porter Wright

00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:55.030
which is a fairly well-regarded firm,

00:11:55.030 --> 00:11:57.460
which is probably why they
left, which we'll get to,

00:11:57.460 --> 00:12:00.150
and another law office of Linda Kerns.

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:02.010
Now, these lawyers filed
the original complaint

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:05.400
but moved to withdraw just four
days later on November 13th.

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:07.730
The judge allowed the Porter
Wright lawyers to leave.

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:09.170
And then on November 15th,

00:12:09.170 --> 00:12:11.740
Trump's new lawyers amended the complaint

00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:14.470
and some Texas lawyers joined the case.

00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:16.760
Linda Kerns is still
representing Trump at this point,

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:18.440
and the amended complaint dropped

00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:20.660
counts three through seven
of the original complaint,

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:22.660
the ones that had all the fraud in them.

00:12:22.660 --> 00:12:24.860
The judge scheduled a
hearing on November 17th,

00:12:24.860 --> 00:12:26.000
and on November 16th,

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:29.060
just one day after the lawyers
filed the amended complaint,

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:31.570
the lawyers filed a motion
to withdraw from the case,

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:33.670
and a third set of lawyers filed a motion

00:12:33.670 --> 00:12:35.480
to continue the hearing
because they had just joined,

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:38.240
which under normal circumstances
would probably happen,

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:40.030
but when you're kind
of playing shenanigans,

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:42.860
the judge is unlikely to
allow that kind of stuff.

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:44.650
And here the judge denied this motion

00:12:44.650 --> 00:12:46.850
because there were
lawyers still on the case

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:48.390
and you can't just muck up the schedule

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:50.800
because you're hiring and firing lawyers.

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:53.540
And on November 17th,
the day of the hearing,

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:55.650
Rudy Giuliani files a petition to appear

00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:57.450
on behalf of the Trump campaign.

00:12:57.450 --> 00:13:01.090
This was the fourth change in
representation in seven days.

00:13:01.090 --> 00:13:02.650
Rudy then attends the hearing,

00:13:02.650 --> 00:13:04.480
which we'll get to because it's hilarious,

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:06.970
and he also files the
second amended complaint.

00:13:06.970 --> 00:13:08.560
But the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:10.810
don't allow plaintiffs
to amend their complaints

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:12.010
an unlimited number of times.

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:15.120
You can't just create a moving
target to avoid dismissal.

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:16.930
Plaintiffs generally get
one amended complaint

00:13:16.930 --> 00:13:19.230
as a matter of right within
a certain number of days,

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:21.830
and then subsequent amendments
require the leave of court,

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:24.440
require discretion of the
court to allow you to do it.

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:25.870
Nevertheless, at the same time,

00:13:25.870 --> 00:13:27.750
the defendants were filing
a motion to dismiss,

00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:29.350
to get rid of this case entirely,

00:13:29.350 --> 00:13:31.600
and Rudy and his team filed responses

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:33.060
to that motion to dismiss

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:35.670
that made new claims that
were outside the four corners

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:37.360
of the first amended complaint,

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:40.800
that there were a grand
scheme to elect Joe Biden

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:42.540
and the voters were treated unequally.

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:44.010
I mean, it's a whole mess,

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:46.540
which leads to the oral argument

00:13:46.540 --> 00:13:48.660
arguing this motion to dismiss.

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:51.500
Rudy Giuliani was there to
try to prevent the court

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:53.700
from dismissing this case out of hand,

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:56.350
and this oral argument
was one for the ages.

00:13:56.350 --> 00:13:59.380
Here Rudy Giuliani had
a hard time grasping

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:01.810
what was in the first amended
complaint, what was deleted,

00:14:01.810 --> 00:14:05.210
and what he was trying
to add by amending again

00:14:05.210 --> 00:14:07.170
to add a second amended complaint.

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:09.740
So here's Rudy arguing that
there was stuff in the complaint

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:11.170
that simply wasn't there.

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:13.270
- [Rudy] The only clarification is

00:14:13.270 --> 00:14:15.000
that we are gonna seek leave,

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:16.690
and we've already prepared it,

00:14:16.690 --> 00:14:18.310
to amend this complaint

00:14:18.310 --> 00:14:21.220
to restore our due process claim

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:23.223
which we think was mistakenly removed.

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:26.510
But we are withdrawing,

00:14:26.510 --> 00:14:27.770
we're preserving for appeal

00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:29.887
the elector and the Elections Clause,

00:14:29.887 --> 00:14:31.380
but we will not be proceeding on that.

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:32.690
- And some of this was in

00:14:32.690 --> 00:14:34.610
the deleted second amended complaint,

00:14:34.610 --> 00:14:35.867
but Rudy basically told the court,

00:14:35.867 --> 00:14:37.620
"My bad. It couldn't be helped.

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:40.590
Changing of a lot of
lawyers. Shrug emoji."

00:14:40.590 --> 00:14:42.990
And there's a crucial
part of this oral argument

00:14:42.990 --> 00:14:44.870
where Rudy has a hard time distinguishing

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:47.370
whether he is alleging fraud
in the amended complaint

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:50.520
or if he isn't arguing fraud
in the amended complaint.

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:51.550
- [Judge Brann] So it's
correct to say then

00:14:51.550 --> 00:14:55.310
that you're not alleging fraud
in the amended complaint?

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:57.580
- [Rudy] No, Your Honor, it is not,

00:14:57.580 --> 00:15:00.623
because we incorporate
by reference, in 150,

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:04.410
all of the allegations that precede it

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:07.069
which include a long explanation

00:15:07.069 --> 00:15:09.223
of a fraudulent process,

00:15:10.370 --> 00:15:12.440
a planned, fraudulent process-

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:13.740
- [Judge Brann] So you
are alleging a fraud?

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:14.590
- Yes, Your Honor.
- And, of course,

00:15:14.590 --> 00:15:17.140
at other times he's arguing
the complete opposite.

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:18.930
- [Judge Brann] Does the amended complaint

00:15:18.930 --> 00:15:21.155
plead fraud with particularity?

00:15:21.155 --> 00:15:23.847
- [Rudy] No, Your Honor,
and it doesn't plead fraud.

00:15:23.847 --> 00:15:25.910
- And the reason that this is so important

00:15:25.910 --> 00:15:28.580
is because that one of the
Rules of Civil Procedure,

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:29.970
Rule 9b,

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:32.040
requires that if you allege fraud,

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:34.450
you have to plead that with
what's called particularity.

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:35.920
Normally when you file a lawsuit,

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:38.760
you can just file
allegations on information

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:40.750
and belief that you think this happened,

00:15:40.750 --> 00:15:42.860
and this is called notice pleading.

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:45.860
But here, if you allege
fraud or a mistake,

00:15:45.860 --> 00:15:49.520
you have to allege the
actual particularized facts

00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:51.140
and you can't get away with just

00:15:51.140 --> 00:15:54.080
pleading these weird, nebulous claims.

00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:56.890
So if he is actually arguing fraud,

00:15:56.890 --> 00:16:00.140
then the standard by which
he has to plead his case

00:16:00.140 --> 00:16:01.490
is much, much higher,

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:03.530
and he obviously doesn't want to do that.

00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:06.390
But Rudy was often confused
about what he actually wanted

00:16:06.390 --> 00:16:08.270
throughout this particular oral argument,

00:16:08.270 --> 00:16:09.950
which takes me to my favorite part

00:16:09.950 --> 00:16:11.510
of this particular hearing,

00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:13.500
where Rudy Giuliani is trying to explain

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:14.730
the standard of law that applies.

00:16:14.730 --> 00:16:16.040
Now remember, for context,

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:17.860
that this is on a motion to dismiss.

00:16:17.860 --> 00:16:19.830
So the defendants have filed a motion

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:22.010
for the court to just throw this case out,

00:16:22.010 --> 00:16:24.810
and when you file such a
motion, generally speaking,

00:16:24.810 --> 00:16:27.620
you have to assume that
every factual allegation

00:16:27.620 --> 00:16:30.780
that the the plaintiffs have pled is true,

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:33.100
so you're only looking at
whether it states a legal claim.

00:16:33.100 --> 00:16:34.760
You're not testing the sufficiency

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:36.750
of the factual allegations.

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:37.960
Now, there's some nuance to that,

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:39.910
but that's generally what's going on.

00:16:39.910 --> 00:16:41.940
But at the same time,
if you, as a plaintiff,

00:16:41.940 --> 00:16:45.620
are arguing that a governmental
law is unconstitutional,

00:16:45.620 --> 00:16:46.750
as they were here,

00:16:46.750 --> 00:16:48.003
then there's also a level of scrutiny

00:16:48.003 --> 00:16:50.310
that the court must apply in terms of

00:16:50.310 --> 00:16:53.960
determining whether this law
is unconstitutional or not.

00:16:53.960 --> 00:16:55.970
So there's the standard
for the motion to dismiss

00:16:55.970 --> 00:16:58.660
and then there's the
standard for examining

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:01.650
whether the law itself
is constitutional or not,

00:17:01.650 --> 00:17:03.550
so that's the setup for
what's happening right here.

00:17:03.550 --> 00:17:06.610
The judge asks Rudy what
standard should be applied.

00:17:06.610 --> 00:17:07.550
- [Judge Brann] Are you arguing then

00:17:07.550 --> 00:17:09.840
that strict scrutiny should apply?

00:17:09.840 --> 00:17:12.450
- [Rudy] No, the normal
scrutiny should apply.

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:15.460
If we had alleged fraud, yes,

00:17:15.460 --> 00:17:17.210
but this is not a fraud cause.

00:17:17.210 --> 00:17:19.140
- And what you can hear
is that there's confusion.

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:20.580
The judge is talking about the scrutiny

00:17:20.580 --> 00:17:22.040
that will be applied to determining

00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:24.240
whether the Pennsylvania
law is constitutional.

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:26.390
Rudy misunderstands and he
thinks that they're talking about

00:17:26.390 --> 00:17:28.590
the standard that will apply
to the motion to dismiss,

00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:30.440
which is why he says normal scrutiny.

00:17:30.440 --> 00:17:32.430
Now, that's not really a thing,

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:34.850
but what he's saying
is that we should give

00:17:34.850 --> 00:17:36.670
the plaintiff the benefit of the doubt,

00:17:36.670 --> 00:17:38.670
assume the factual allegations are true,

00:17:38.670 --> 00:17:41.070
and that's the standard
on the motion to dismiss.

00:17:41.070 --> 00:17:43.410
And it's not crazy that
you would talk about

00:17:43.410 --> 00:17:44.450
these two different standards.

00:17:44.450 --> 00:17:46.420
It's ships passing in the night.

00:17:46.420 --> 00:17:47.550
That's understandable,

00:17:47.550 --> 00:17:49.850
but what we're about to
hear is totally inexcusable.

00:17:49.850 --> 00:17:51.870
The judge continues to
talk about the scrutiny,

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:54.350
the level of scrutiny
towards the Pennsylvania law,

00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:56.460
and Rudy just misses the mark entirely.

00:17:56.460 --> 00:17:58.618
- [Judge Brann] Why don't
Secretary Boockvar's

00:17:58.618 --> 00:18:00.180
and the county's

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:02.640
satisfy the standard review
that you're talking about?

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:04.310
If it's not strict scrutiny

00:18:04.310 --> 00:18:06.550
and it's the standard
review you're implying,

00:18:06.550 --> 00:18:08.990
why don't their actions satisfy this?

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:11.190
- [Rudy] I'm sorry,

00:18:11.190 --> 00:18:13.230
I don't really understand
the question, Your Honor.

00:18:13.230 --> 00:18:14.350
- [Judge Brann] Well, this
is how I would look at it.

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:16.300
I would think that it's a standard review

00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:18.530
of strict scrutiny, essentially.

00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:19.840
You're not sure that that's the case.

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:21.340
I'm not imposing my-

00:18:21.340 --> 00:18:23.710
- [Rudy] Maybe I don't understand
what you mean by scrutiny.

00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:26.500
- So here the judge is
clearly talking about

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:29.160
how he should analyze the
Pennsylvania law itself,

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:31.510
not the standard on the motion to dismiss.

00:18:31.510 --> 00:18:33.670
Generally there's three types of scrutiny

00:18:33.670 --> 00:18:36.390
when a court is analyzing a
law for constitutionality.

00:18:36.390 --> 00:18:38.140
One is rational basis review,

00:18:38.140 --> 00:18:40.990
which is how courts
generally look at laws.

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:43.060
It's assumed that as
long as the legislature

00:18:43.060 --> 00:18:44.550
has any rational basis,

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:45.960
that law will be upheld.

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:47.350
Then there are certain times

00:18:47.350 --> 00:18:48.720
when you use intermediate scrutiny,

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:50.750
and then there's what's
called strict scrutiny,

00:18:50.750 --> 00:18:54.420
where if you interfere
with a fundamental right,

00:18:54.420 --> 00:18:57.463
the court will almost
always strike it down.

00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:00.040
That's why it's called strict scrutiny.

00:19:00.040 --> 00:19:02.610
You're really scrutinizing
that particular law.

00:19:02.610 --> 00:19:05.960
And what's crazy about this
is that, as the plaintiff,

00:19:05.960 --> 00:19:08.880
the Trump campaign is saying
that these laws interfere

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:11.600
with the voting rights of
people in Pennsylvania,

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:14.370
so they want strict scrutiny to apply.

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:16.900
That makes it easier to
get this law disregarded

00:19:16.900 --> 00:19:19.300
and disregard these particular votes,

00:19:19.300 --> 00:19:21.180
but Rudy totally misses the mark

00:19:21.180 --> 00:19:24.460
and doesn't argue in favor
of using strict scrutiny.

00:19:24.460 --> 00:19:26.480
He has no idea what he's talking about.

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:28.210
And the difference between strict scrutiny

00:19:28.210 --> 00:19:29.370
and rational basis review

00:19:29.370 --> 00:19:30.850
is literally something that you learn

00:19:30.850 --> 00:19:33.550
on the first day of law school.

00:19:33.550 --> 00:19:34.780
And in preparation for this,

00:19:34.780 --> 00:19:36.920
I actually looked at my law school notes

00:19:36.920 --> 00:19:39.320
and that is a bit of hyperbole.

00:19:39.320 --> 00:19:41.560
In my class, we didn't
do rational basis review

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:44.460
versus strict scrutiny until
the second day of law school,

00:19:44.460 --> 00:19:48.210
so it's a bit of hyperbole but not much.

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:49.330
Now, after this hearing,

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:51.577
the Trump campaign said that
Rudy did a fantastic job

00:19:51.577 --> 00:19:54.820
and that he clearly built up
a rapport with Judge Brann,

00:19:54.820 --> 00:19:57.400
who was the judge here
in this particular case,

00:19:57.400 --> 00:20:00.720
because they were talking
about what restaurants to go to

00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:02.140
and making small talk,

00:20:02.140 --> 00:20:03.750
but they changed their tune

00:20:03.750 --> 00:20:07.140
when the judge issued
his ruling on Saturday,

00:20:07.140 --> 00:20:09.950
which I generally refrain
from going through

00:20:09.950 --> 00:20:12.820
actual court decisions on this channel,

00:20:12.820 --> 00:20:15.120
but this decision is very accessible

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:17.800
and is a masterclass in
basic civil procedure,

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:19.150
probably because this case

00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:21.040
was so terrible in the first place.

00:20:21.040 --> 00:20:23.040
But let's start with a
couple of different excerpts

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:24.370
from this particular case,

00:20:24.370 --> 00:20:25.730
starting with the introduction

00:20:25.730 --> 00:20:27.350
because it does a great job of laying out

00:20:27.350 --> 00:20:29.597
what the stakes are in
this particular litigation.

00:20:29.597 --> 00:20:30.727
"In this action, the Trump Campaign

00:20:30.727 --> 00:20:32.100
and the Individual Plaintiffs

00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:33.530
seek to discard millions of votes

00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:36.010
legally cast by Pennsylvanians
from all corners,

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:38.800
from Greene County to Pike
County and everywhere in between.

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:40.490
In other words, Plaintiffs
asked this Court

00:20:40.490 --> 00:20:43.140
to disenfranchise almost 7 million voters.

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:44.990
This Court has been
unable to find any case

00:20:44.990 --> 00:20:47.220
in which a plaintiff has
sought such a drastic remedy

00:20:47.220 --> 00:20:49.090
in the contest of an election,

00:20:49.090 --> 00:20:52.020
in terms of sheer volume of
votes asked to be invalidated.

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:54.930
One might expect that when
seeking such a startling outcome,

00:20:54.930 --> 00:20:56.880
a plaintiff would come formidably armed

00:20:56.880 --> 00:20:58.190
with compelling legal arguments

00:20:58.190 --> 00:21:00.590
and factual proof of rampant corruption,

00:21:00.590 --> 00:21:02.270
such that this Court would have no option

00:21:02.270 --> 00:21:05.100
but to regrettably grant the
proposed injunctive relief

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:06.460
despite the impact that it would have

00:21:06.460 --> 00:21:08.290
on such a large group of citizens.

00:21:08.290 --> 00:21:09.810
This has not happened.

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:11.330
Instead, this Court has been presented

00:21:11.330 --> 00:21:13.100
with strained legal
arguments without merit

00:21:13.100 --> 00:21:14.550
and speculative accusations,

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:16.170
unpled in the operative complaint

00:21:16.170 --> 00:21:17.800
and unsupported by evidence.

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:18.930
In the United States of America,

00:21:18.930 --> 00:21:20.960
this cannot justify the disenfranchisement

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:22.030
of a single voter,

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:23.340
let alone all of the voters

00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:25.640
of its sixth most populated state.

00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:27.990
Our people, laws, and
institutions demand more.

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:30.310
At bottom, Plaintiffs have
failed to meet their burden

00:21:30.310 --> 00:21:32.490
to state a claim upon which
relief may be granted.

00:21:32.490 --> 00:21:34.080
Therefore, I grant Defendants' motion

00:21:34.080 --> 00:21:36.410
and dismiss Plaintiffs'
action with prejudice."

00:21:36.410 --> 00:21:38.540
And then the court goes
into a great summary of

00:21:38.540 --> 00:21:40.350
what actually happened in this case.

00:21:40.350 --> 00:21:42.800
Here in 2019,

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:44.940
Pennsylvania allowed mail voting

00:21:44.940 --> 00:21:47.830
for all of the people in Pennsylvania.

00:21:47.830 --> 00:21:51.770
They allowed for various
counties to provide

00:21:51.770 --> 00:21:54.670
for curing of the ballots
if there was a defect,

00:21:54.670 --> 00:21:56.240
but in a prior case,

00:21:56.240 --> 00:22:00.330
no counties were required
to adopt a cure process

00:22:00.330 --> 00:22:02.930
so different counties
have different rules.

00:22:02.930 --> 00:22:05.380
And here, the two
plaintiffs were in counties

00:22:05.380 --> 00:22:08.040
where they didn't have an
opportunity to cure their ballots

00:22:08.040 --> 00:22:09.860
and they didn't cure their ballots.

00:22:09.860 --> 00:22:11.650
And then the court goes
on to actually analyze

00:22:11.650 --> 00:22:14.167
the legal merit of the
plaintiffs' claims stating that,

00:22:14.167 --> 00:22:16.250
"Plaintiffs' only remaining claim alleges

00:22:16.250 --> 00:22:17.700
a violation of equal protection.

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:19.420
This claim, like Frankenstein's Monster,

00:22:19.420 --> 00:22:21.210
has been haphazardly stitched together

00:22:21.210 --> 00:22:22.690
from two distinct legal theories

00:22:22.690 --> 00:22:24.810
in an attempt to avoid
controlling precedent.

00:22:24.810 --> 00:22:26.140
The general thrust of this claim is

00:22:26.140 --> 00:22:28.230
that it is unconstitutional
for Pennsylvania

00:22:28.230 --> 00:22:29.660
to give states discretion

00:22:29.660 --> 00:22:31.820
to adopt a notice-and-cure policy."

00:22:31.820 --> 00:22:34.450
In other words, different
counties have different rules.

00:22:34.450 --> 00:22:36.037
And it goes on to state that the

00:22:36.037 --> 00:22:38.310
"Plaintiffs are trying
to mix and match claims

00:22:38.310 --> 00:22:41.000
and bypass contrary precedent
is not lost on the Court."

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:43.260
And then the court goes on to
analyze the standing issue,

00:22:43.260 --> 00:22:45.780
and this is something that
often gets lost in litigation,

00:22:45.780 --> 00:22:48.810
but a plaintiff actually has
to have suffered an injury.

00:22:48.810 --> 00:22:51.570
You can't just go to court
to get an advisory opinion

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:53.450
if you haven't been personally wronged

00:22:53.450 --> 00:22:55.210
in a legally cognizable way.

00:22:55.210 --> 00:22:56.980
And here, the court goes on to say

00:22:56.980 --> 00:23:00.500
that effectively their votes were rejected

00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:03.380
as were the rules of
those particular counties

00:23:03.380 --> 00:23:06.000
and different counties
can have different rules,

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:08.590
and that the Trump campaign is not a voter

00:23:08.590 --> 00:23:10.310
with a rejected vote at all

00:23:10.310 --> 00:23:13.930
so the Trump campaign doesn't
have a standing at all.

00:23:13.930 --> 00:23:15.650
And then the court gets to the heart

00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:17.370
of the equal protection argument,

00:23:17.370 --> 00:23:19.620
and because there's so much
misunderstanding about that,

00:23:19.620 --> 00:23:20.820
I think it's worth going over here

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:23.110
because the court summary
is really fantastic.

00:23:23.110 --> 00:23:24.237
The court says,

00:23:24.237 --> 00:23:25.670
"Contrary to Plaintiffs' assertions,

00:23:25.670 --> 00:23:28.910
not all unequal treatment
requires Court intervention.

00:23:28.910 --> 00:23:31.530
The Equal Protection Clause
does not forbid classifications.

00:23:31.530 --> 00:23:33.900
It simply keeps
governmental decision-makers

00:23:33.900 --> 00:23:36.970
from treating similarly
situated persons differently.

00:23:36.970 --> 00:23:38.150
The government could not function

00:23:38.150 --> 00:23:41.340
if complete equality were
required in all situations.

00:23:41.340 --> 00:23:45.480
Consequently, a classification
resulting in some inequality

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:47.250
will be upheld unless it is based on

00:23:47.250 --> 00:23:49.570
an inherently suspect characterization

00:23:49.570 --> 00:23:53.050
or jeopardizes the exercise
of a fundamental right."

00:23:53.050 --> 00:23:55.010
So for example,

00:23:55.010 --> 00:23:58.270
if you're a store and
you want to reject people

00:23:58.270 --> 00:24:00.380
who don't have a shirt or shoes on,

00:24:00.380 --> 00:24:01.213
you can do that.

00:24:01.213 --> 00:24:02.890
That's a classification of people.

00:24:02.890 --> 00:24:04.370
You're allowed to prevent those people

00:24:04.370 --> 00:24:05.710
from shopping at your store,

00:24:05.710 --> 00:24:09.150
but you cannot prevent people
from shopping at your store

00:24:09.150 --> 00:24:11.620
on the basis that their
skin color is different.

00:24:11.620 --> 00:24:13.400
That is a fundamental right

00:24:13.400 --> 00:24:15.400
and it's a difference between people

00:24:15.400 --> 00:24:20.400
that is recognized as a
inherently suspect classification.

00:24:20.500 --> 00:24:22.140
So let's connect that concept

00:24:22.140 --> 00:24:23.530
with what the plaintiffs alleged here

00:24:23.530 --> 00:24:25.750
regarding their votes and
being treated differently.

00:24:25.750 --> 00:24:27.217
And the court goes on to say,

00:24:27.217 --> 00:24:30.560
"Here, because Defendants'
conduct imposes no burden

00:24:30.560 --> 00:24:32.410
on individual Plaintiffs' right to vote,

00:24:32.410 --> 00:24:34.030
their equal-protection claim is subject

00:24:34.030 --> 00:24:35.670
to rational basis review."

00:24:35.670 --> 00:24:37.527
Going back to the scrutiny level.

00:24:37.527 --> 00:24:38.590
"Defendant Counties,

00:24:38.590 --> 00:24:40.970
by implementing a
notice-and-cure procedure,

00:24:40.970 --> 00:24:44.230
have in fact lifted a
burden on the right to vote,

00:24:44.230 --> 00:24:46.880
even if only for those who
live in those counties."

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:47.950
In other words,

00:24:47.950 --> 00:24:50.300
these plaintiffs are
arguing that it's not fair

00:24:50.300 --> 00:24:52.690
that people in the other counties,

00:24:52.690 --> 00:24:54.530
especially around Philadelphia,

00:24:54.530 --> 00:24:57.260
were allowed to cure their defective votes

00:24:57.260 --> 00:24:58.800
when they lived in counties

00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:01.390
that did not allow for a cure procedure,

00:25:01.390 --> 00:25:03.080
but that's not how that works.

00:25:03.080 --> 00:25:05.220
Number one, the people
in those other counties

00:25:05.220 --> 00:25:08.570
had a restriction lifted,

00:25:08.570 --> 00:25:10.300
and it's okay either way.

00:25:10.300 --> 00:25:12.170
A county can say,
"You're allowed to cure,"

00:25:12.170 --> 00:25:14.480
and a county can also say,
"You're not allowed to cure,"

00:25:14.480 --> 00:25:17.120
but you can't argue that you were injured

00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:18.550
because you didn't live in a county

00:25:18.550 --> 00:25:20.550
that allowed you to cure your ballot.

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:21.840
That's just craziness.

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:24.120
And often the level of
scrutiny that the court applies

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:25.550
is the whole game.

00:25:25.550 --> 00:25:26.750
If it's rational basis review,

00:25:26.750 --> 00:25:28.090
the law is probably gonna be upheld,

00:25:28.090 --> 00:25:29.160
and if it's strict scrutiny,

00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:30.800
the law will probably be struck.

00:25:30.800 --> 00:25:32.107
And the court goes on to say,

00:25:32.107 --> 00:25:33.880
"Individual Plaintiffs' claims fail

00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:36.130
because it's perfectly
rational for a state to provide

00:25:36.130 --> 00:25:38.310
counties discretion to notify voters

00:25:38.310 --> 00:25:41.700
that they may cure procedurally
defective mail-in ballots."

00:25:41.700 --> 00:25:42.977
And the court goes on,

00:25:42.977 --> 00:25:45.010
"Even assuming that they can establish

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:46.680
that their right to vote had been denied,

00:25:46.680 --> 00:25:47.560
which they cannot,

00:25:47.560 --> 00:25:50.140
Plaintiffs seek to remedy
the denial of their votes

00:25:50.140 --> 00:25:52.820
by invalidating the votes
of millions of others.

00:25:52.820 --> 00:25:55.300
Rather than requesting that
their votes be counted,

00:25:55.300 --> 00:25:57.870
they seek to discredit
scores of other votes,

00:25:57.870 --> 00:25:59.420
but only for one race.

00:25:59.420 --> 00:26:01.530
This is simply not how
the Constitution works."

00:26:01.530 --> 00:26:03.700
And the race that they're referring to is

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:04.620
the presidential race.

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:07.150
They want to disregard the votes

00:26:07.150 --> 00:26:08.430
with respect to the president

00:26:08.430 --> 00:26:10.590
but not the other down-ballot votes.

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:12.990
This is what the court
refers to as leveling down

00:26:12.990 --> 00:26:14.460
as opposed to leveling up.

00:26:14.460 --> 00:26:17.190
If you leveled up, you'd allow
everyone's vote to be counted

00:26:17.190 --> 00:26:18.920
or at least to be cured,

00:26:18.920 --> 00:26:21.950
but they are asking the
court to level down everyone

00:26:21.950 --> 00:26:24.570
and disregard all of these
other votes that were made

00:26:24.570 --> 00:26:26.560
that were allowed to be cured.

00:26:26.560 --> 00:26:28.000
And then the court goes on to say

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:31.130
that the allegations with
respect to poll watchers,

00:26:31.130 --> 00:26:32.540
they didn't actually allege

00:26:32.540 --> 00:26:34.890
that the Republican poll
watchers were treated differently

00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:36.220
than the Democratic poll watchers,

00:26:36.220 --> 00:26:38.870
so there's no possible equal protection

00:26:38.870 --> 00:26:40.600
or voting impropriety there.

00:26:40.600 --> 00:26:42.537
And then in the conclusion the court says,

00:26:42.537 --> 00:26:44.670
"Defendants' motion to dismiss
the First Amended Complaint

00:26:44.670 --> 00:26:46.230
are granted with prejudice.

00:26:46.230 --> 00:26:48.110
Leave to amend is denied."

00:26:48.110 --> 00:26:49.210
Now, I want to point that out

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:51.940
because there were a lot
of people on the internet

00:26:51.940 --> 00:26:54.000
who pointed to the with prejudice language

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:55.930
as being particularly significant.

00:26:55.930 --> 00:26:58.933
Even really seasoned lawyers
got this one screwed up.

00:26:59.960 --> 00:27:02.600
What I will say is that
the with prejudice language

00:27:02.600 --> 00:27:05.060
is really not unusual at all.

00:27:05.060 --> 00:27:06.980
All that means is that the court thinks

00:27:06.980 --> 00:27:08.560
that allowing the plaintiff

00:27:08.560 --> 00:27:11.880
to add additional factual
allegations would be futile.

00:27:11.880 --> 00:27:14.500
There's nothing that they can plausibly do

00:27:14.500 --> 00:27:17.470
to be able to state an
actual valid legal claim

00:27:17.470 --> 00:27:19.690
and so that's why the court
is denying leave to amend,

00:27:19.690 --> 00:27:21.200
because there's nothing that they can do.

00:27:21.200 --> 00:27:22.940
The claims are gonna fail no matter what.

00:27:22.940 --> 00:27:24.550
And with prejudice simply means

00:27:24.550 --> 00:27:27.650
that the decision is being
made on the merits of the case

00:27:27.650 --> 00:27:30.210
and that there's no leave to amend.

00:27:30.210 --> 00:27:33.170
So with prejudice is
something that you see in

00:27:33.170 --> 00:27:36.380
basically every civil
case in the entire world.

00:27:36.380 --> 00:27:39.020
It doesn't actually carry
any more significance

00:27:39.020 --> 00:27:42.810
other than this case is now
over at the trial court level.

00:27:42.810 --> 00:27:44.960
They can now appeal the decision,

00:27:44.960 --> 00:27:48.100
but it's over at the trial court.

00:27:48.100 --> 00:27:49.810
But it does appear that the Trump campaign

00:27:49.810 --> 00:27:51.300
is going to appeal

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:53.112
this particular decision.
(screen chimes)

00:27:53.112 --> 00:27:55.810
Ooh, it's Devin from the future again,

00:27:55.810 --> 00:27:57.100
and I will tell you,

00:27:57.100 --> 00:27:59.000
I have seen some stuff.

00:27:59.000 --> 00:28:01.550
But, interestingly, since
I am from the future,

00:28:01.550 --> 00:28:02.810
I can tell you that the Trump campaign

00:28:02.810 --> 00:28:05.090
did in fact file an appeal,

00:28:05.090 --> 00:28:06.840
and the Third Circuit Court of Appeals

00:28:06.840 --> 00:28:09.850
heard the appeal in basically
record time and rejected it.

00:28:09.850 --> 00:28:10.960
They issued their appeal

00:28:10.960 --> 00:28:12.650
even before I could get this video out,

00:28:12.650 --> 00:28:14.650
so I had to re-shoot this pickup,

00:28:14.650 --> 00:28:16.850
but the Court of Appeals basically said

00:28:16.850 --> 00:28:18.440
that the trial court, Judge Brann,

00:28:18.440 --> 00:28:20.650
got this decision exactly right

00:28:20.650 --> 00:28:23.487
and rejected the Trump
campaign's appeal stating,

00:28:23.487 --> 00:28:26.990
"Free, fair elections are the
lifeblood of our democracy.

00:28:26.990 --> 00:28:29.030
Charges of unfairness are serious.

00:28:29.030 --> 00:28:31.750
But calling an election
unfair does not make it so.

00:28:31.750 --> 00:28:34.560
Charges require specific
allegations and then proof.

00:28:34.560 --> 00:28:36.120
We have neither here."

00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:37.940
And the Court of Appeals
actually calls out

00:28:37.940 --> 00:28:39.417
Rudy Giuliani by name stating,

00:28:39.417 --> 00:28:41.590
"But as lawyer Rudolph Giuliani stressed,

00:28:41.590 --> 00:28:43.090
the Campaign 'doesn't plead fraud.

00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:44.770
This is not a fraud case.'

00:28:44.770 --> 00:28:47.100
Instead, it objects that
Pennsylvania's Secretary of State

00:28:47.100 --> 00:28:49.120
and some counties restricted poll watchers

00:28:49.120 --> 00:28:51.070
and let voters fix technical defects

00:28:51.070 --> 00:28:52.520
in their mail-in ballots.

00:28:52.520 --> 00:28:54.080
It offers nothing more.

00:28:54.080 --> 00:28:56.410
This case is not about
whether those claims are true.

00:28:56.410 --> 00:28:59.260
Rather, the Campaign appeals
on a very narrow ground:

00:28:59.260 --> 00:29:01.770
whether the District Court
abused its discretion

00:29:01.770 --> 00:29:03.160
in not letting the Campaign

00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:05.150
amend its complaint a second time.

00:29:05.150 --> 00:29:06.660
It did not."

00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:08.060
This is a case that the Trump campaign

00:29:08.060 --> 00:29:09.330
was never going to win,

00:29:09.330 --> 00:29:11.930
but it's interesting because this might be

00:29:11.930 --> 00:29:13.010
one of the rare instances

00:29:13.010 --> 00:29:15.170
where a particular lawyer's bad lawyering

00:29:15.170 --> 00:29:16.920
actually made things worse,

00:29:16.920 --> 00:29:18.730
and it appears that that's what happened

00:29:18.730 --> 00:29:21.010
with some of Rudy's admissions,

00:29:21.010 --> 00:29:22.390
or at least his lawyering.

00:29:22.390 --> 00:29:23.980
And the Third Circuit Court of Appeals,

00:29:23.980 --> 00:29:26.610
in an opinion authored
by a Trump appointee,

00:29:26.610 --> 00:29:28.500
points out that the only issue

00:29:28.500 --> 00:29:30.370
that the Trump campaign appealed on

00:29:30.370 --> 00:29:32.490
is this very narrow issue
of whether the trial court

00:29:32.490 --> 00:29:34.980
abused its discretion in not allowing them

00:29:34.980 --> 00:29:36.990
to amend the complaint yet again,

00:29:36.990 --> 00:29:39.310
and for reasons that we already covered,

00:29:39.310 --> 00:29:41.480
that was really not going to win anyway.

00:29:41.480 --> 00:29:44.140
But in many ways the Trump campaign has

00:29:44.140 --> 00:29:46.140
given up a whole bunch of different issues

00:29:46.140 --> 00:29:47.050
that they could have raised

00:29:47.050 --> 00:29:50.040
and just barreled forward
with this one narrow issue

00:29:50.040 --> 00:29:51.740
that's not going to change the day.

00:29:51.740 --> 00:29:52.950
And the Third Circuit Court of Appeals

00:29:52.950 --> 00:29:54.990
basically comes as close as they ever will

00:29:54.990 --> 00:29:56.340
to saying that this was all based on

00:29:56.340 --> 00:29:58.380
the Chewbacca defense in the first place,

00:29:58.380 --> 00:30:00.100
where this court actually compares

00:30:00.100 --> 00:30:01.807
the Trump effort to alchemy stating,

00:30:01.807 --> 00:30:04.270
"Seeking to turn those state-law
claims into federal ones,

00:30:04.270 --> 00:30:06.070
the Campaign claims discrimination.

00:30:06.070 --> 00:30:09.100
But its alchemy cannot
transmute lead into gold.

00:30:09.100 --> 00:30:11.050
The Campaign never alleges that any ballot

00:30:11.050 --> 00:30:13.660
was fraudulent or cast
by any illegal voter.

00:30:13.660 --> 00:30:15.190
It never alleges that any defendant

00:30:15.190 --> 00:30:17.180
treated the Trump campaign or its votes

00:30:17.180 --> 00:30:20.330
worse than it treated the
Biden campaign or its votes.

00:30:20.330 --> 00:30:22.430
Calling something discrimination
does not make it so.

00:30:22.430 --> 00:30:24.030
The Second Amended Complaint still suffers

00:30:24.030 --> 00:30:25.540
from these core defects,

00:30:25.540 --> 00:30:28.350
so granting leave to amend
would have been futile."

00:30:28.350 --> 00:30:30.460
- That does not make sense.

00:30:30.460 --> 00:30:31.650
- And now the case is out,

00:30:31.650 --> 00:30:34.670
but obviously they can try to
appeal to the Supreme Court.

00:30:34.670 --> 00:30:35.950
The Third Circuit Court of Appeals

00:30:35.950 --> 00:30:38.130
has to hear the appeal no matter what,

00:30:38.130 --> 00:30:40.640
but the Supreme Court is based
on what's called certiorari,

00:30:40.640 --> 00:30:42.730
so it's completely up to the Supreme Court

00:30:42.730 --> 00:30:44.500
whether they will want to hear any part

00:30:44.500 --> 00:30:46.520
of this particular case,

00:30:46.520 --> 00:30:49.690
and 99% chance that the Supreme Court

00:30:49.690 --> 00:30:51.090
rejects this out of hand,

00:30:51.090 --> 00:30:52.360
or if they do take it up,

00:30:52.360 --> 00:30:54.450
it will probably be a quick dismissal.

00:30:54.450 --> 00:30:57.260
So the Trump campaign is
probably out of luck here

00:30:57.260 --> 00:30:59.120
and there's not a whole lot
that the Supreme Court could do

00:30:59.120 --> 00:31:00.890
even if they picked up this case.

00:31:00.890 --> 00:31:01.723
Now, at the same time,

00:31:01.723 --> 00:31:03.380
I saw a lot of people being worried about

00:31:03.380 --> 00:31:06.290
the decision in Kelly versus Pennsylvania.

00:31:06.290 --> 00:31:08.700
In that lawsuit, several
Republican candidates and voters

00:31:08.700 --> 00:31:10.860
filed a lawsuit against Pennsylvania,

00:31:10.860 --> 00:31:12.190
the general assembly and the governor

00:31:12.190 --> 00:31:13.470
and the Secretary of State.

00:31:13.470 --> 00:31:15.090
The lawsuit was to block the state

00:31:15.090 --> 00:31:16.770
from certifying the election results

00:31:16.770 --> 00:31:19.420
and asked the courts to
exclude mail-in ballots

00:31:19.420 --> 00:31:20.350
from the certified results,

00:31:20.350 --> 00:31:22.840
or alternatively to direct
the general assembly

00:31:22.840 --> 00:31:24.340
to choose its own electors.

00:31:24.340 --> 00:31:26.250
And the media reported

00:31:26.250 --> 00:31:27.930
that the Commonwealth Court

00:31:27.930 --> 00:31:30.370
stopped certification of the votes,

00:31:30.370 --> 00:31:31.510
which actually is not true.

00:31:31.510 --> 00:31:33.477
The court's order merely said, quote,

00:31:33.477 --> 00:31:35.270
"to the extent that there remains

00:31:35.270 --> 00:31:37.440
any further action to
perfect certification

00:31:37.440 --> 00:31:39.760
of the results of the
2020 General Election

00:31:39.760 --> 00:31:41.880
for presidents and vice president,

00:31:41.880 --> 00:31:44.030
certification was enjoined,"

00:31:44.030 --> 00:31:45.530
but there was no further action needed

00:31:45.530 --> 00:31:46.770
to perfect certification.

00:31:46.770 --> 00:31:48.660
It already was certified.

00:31:48.660 --> 00:31:50.540
And on top of that, the
Pennsylvania Supreme Court

00:31:50.540 --> 00:31:52.430
has already vacated the order.

00:31:52.430 --> 00:31:55.000
This Kelly versus Pennsylvania
suit is pretty much

00:31:55.000 --> 00:31:56.540
much ado about nothing.

00:31:56.540 --> 00:31:59.030
And the beclowning
continued in another case,

00:31:59.030 --> 00:32:01.010
in Wood versus Reffensperger.

00:32:01.010 --> 00:32:03.150
This is a case where the provided evidence

00:32:03.150 --> 00:32:05.050
was asserting all kinds of voter fraud

00:32:05.050 --> 00:32:06.800
in different counties in Michigan

00:32:06.800 --> 00:32:09.000
that were actually in Minnesota,

00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:12.300
and the court issued a complete
smackdown denying the TRO,

00:32:12.300 --> 00:32:13.780
the temporary restraining order,

00:32:13.780 --> 00:32:16.050
and went so far as to say that
the plaintiff in that case

00:32:16.050 --> 00:32:19.210
was using authority that
was no longer good law,

00:32:19.210 --> 00:32:20.320
that the Eleventh Circuit

00:32:20.320 --> 00:32:22.210
had expressly abrogated that holding,

00:32:22.210 --> 00:32:24.760
and yet the plaintiff was
using that as a reason

00:32:24.760 --> 00:32:25.957
why the court should act here.

00:32:25.957 --> 00:32:28.700
And the court also said that
the case was barred by laches,

00:32:28.700 --> 00:32:30.340
which is an issue in a lot of

00:32:30.340 --> 00:32:31.944
these different election cases.

00:32:31.944 --> 00:32:35.010
The doctrine of laches
is an unnecessary delay,

00:32:35.010 --> 00:32:37.847
so if you delay too long
in bringing your case

00:32:37.847 --> 00:32:39.320
and the circumstances change,

00:32:39.320 --> 00:32:41.530
then you shouldn't be
allowed to go forward

00:32:41.530 --> 00:32:42.940
with your particular lawsuit.

00:32:42.940 --> 00:32:45.050
And when you're talking
about election law,

00:32:45.050 --> 00:32:48.010
so many of these laws regarding

00:32:48.010 --> 00:32:49.990
the way that the votes
are going to be counted

00:32:49.990 --> 00:32:53.040
or the way that the election
is going to go forward,

00:32:53.040 --> 00:32:55.710
those laws existed before the election.

00:32:55.710 --> 00:32:58.100
So it's not fair that
you get to hang back,

00:32:58.100 --> 00:32:59.730
wait to see the results of the election,

00:32:59.730 --> 00:33:02.750
and then claim that the
election itself was improper.

00:33:02.750 --> 00:33:04.920
And the court here does
go so far as to say

00:33:04.920 --> 00:33:06.760
that laches should apply

00:33:06.760 --> 00:33:08.900
because you can't say that you don't like

00:33:08.900 --> 00:33:09.810
the result of the election

00:33:09.810 --> 00:33:12.520
and then claim that you
should disregard the election

00:33:12.520 --> 00:33:13.810
entirely after the fact

00:33:13.810 --> 00:33:15.730
when the laws have been
there the whole time.

00:33:15.730 --> 00:33:16.710
And then on top of that,

00:33:16.710 --> 00:33:18.890
the Trump campaign is
voluntarily dismissing

00:33:18.890 --> 00:33:21.990
a whole bunch of cases,
including Pirkle versus Wolf,

00:33:21.990 --> 00:33:23.200
Brooks versus Mahoney,

00:33:23.200 --> 00:33:24.710
Bally versus Whitmer,

00:33:24.710 --> 00:33:26.000
Langenhorst versus Peocor

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:28.190
and Donald Trump versus Benson.

00:33:28.190 --> 00:33:30.880
And finally, that brings us
to the almost mythical claims

00:33:30.880 --> 00:33:32.450
of Sidney Powell.

00:33:32.450 --> 00:33:35.420
We talked earlier about how
she was basically kicked out

00:33:35.420 --> 00:33:38.410
of the Trump campaign's lawsuit strategy,

00:33:38.410 --> 00:33:40.070
but she came back in force,

00:33:40.070 --> 00:33:43.310
filing a lawsuit in Michigan
called King versus Whitmer

00:33:43.310 --> 00:33:45.910
as well as one in Georgia
called Pearson versus Kemp.

00:33:45.910 --> 00:33:47.320
And here,

00:33:47.320 --> 00:33:50.960
these are some of the
most typo-ridden pleadings

00:33:50.960 --> 00:33:53.080
I've ever seen in my entire life,

00:33:53.080 --> 00:33:54.670
and that's just the tip of the iceberg

00:33:54.670 --> 00:33:58.090
when it comes to the procedural
and substantive defects

00:33:58.090 --> 00:33:59.880
of these particular cases.

00:33:59.880 --> 00:34:03.240
Here they're using
evidence from affidavits

00:34:03.240 --> 00:34:05.550
from other cases that have
already been rejected.

00:34:05.550 --> 00:34:09.250
They try to use a star witness

00:34:09.250 --> 00:34:11.910
whose name and background
have been redacted

00:34:11.910 --> 00:34:14.510
without trying to file
anything under seal,

00:34:14.510 --> 00:34:17.520
without going into the weeds
of federal civil procedure.

00:34:17.520 --> 00:34:20.450
That's not how you would
protect someone's identity.

00:34:20.450 --> 00:34:21.670
Not that this is even a place

00:34:21.670 --> 00:34:23.960
where you could protect someone's identity

00:34:23.960 --> 00:34:26.250
from a public filing in
this particular case.

00:34:26.250 --> 00:34:29.220
And these lawsuits are likely
to suffer the exact same fates

00:34:29.220 --> 00:34:31.160
as all of the other
Trump campaign lawsuits

00:34:31.160 --> 00:34:32.380
that we've already seen.

00:34:32.380 --> 00:34:33.500
They're pleading fraud without

00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:35.160
particularized allegations of fact.

00:34:35.160 --> 00:34:36.890
They are seeking relief from the courts.

00:34:36.890 --> 00:34:40.480
That's already moot or impossible
for the courts to grant.

00:34:40.480 --> 00:34:44.250
They're saying that simply
choosing the Dominion software

00:34:44.250 --> 00:34:46.800
was a violation of the Constitution.

00:34:46.800 --> 00:34:48.840
They suffer from standing issues.

00:34:48.840 --> 00:34:52.500
They failed to allege a whole
bunch of causes of action.

00:34:52.500 --> 00:34:54.630
It's just more nonsense

00:34:54.630 --> 00:34:59.050
based on facts that appear
to be completely made up.

00:34:59.050 --> 00:35:00.830
And, of course, this is
happening while the states

00:35:00.830 --> 00:35:03.040
are just simply going on
with business as usual,

00:35:03.040 --> 00:35:06.450
where the crucial states are
just certifying their elections

00:35:06.450 --> 00:35:08.760
and selecting the electors to
go to the electoral college,

00:35:08.760 --> 00:35:11.350
including Nevada, Michigan,
Pennsylvania and Georgia.

00:35:11.350 --> 00:35:14.160
All of those states have
already certified their results,

00:35:14.160 --> 00:35:16.810
and while it's not
impossible to undo that,

00:35:16.810 --> 00:35:18.890
it would be extremely difficult.

00:35:18.890 --> 00:35:20.570
And those actions make these lawsuits,

00:35:20.570 --> 00:35:23.390
and a lot of the other
Trump campaign lawsuits

00:35:23.390 --> 00:35:25.230
to the extent that they exist anymore,

00:35:25.230 --> 00:35:28.410
moot and extremely likely to
be dismissed by the courts.

00:35:28.410 --> 00:35:30.010
It seems that they're
dismissing their cases

00:35:30.010 --> 00:35:31.380
because the allegations of

00:35:31.380 --> 00:35:33.710
grand conspiracies never materialized.

00:35:33.710 --> 00:35:35.920
Sadly, we never saw the Kraken

00:35:35.920 --> 00:35:37.300
that Sidney Powell promised us

00:35:37.300 --> 00:35:39.650
because the Kraken never existed.

00:35:39.650 --> 00:35:41.030
Or maybe the legal Kraken was

00:35:41.030 --> 00:35:42.730
the friends that we made along the way,

00:35:42.730 --> 00:35:44.410
because everything that Sidney Powell

00:35:44.410 --> 00:35:46.630
has been complaining about
has been completely made up,

00:35:46.630 --> 00:35:47.930
from the Dominion voting machines,

00:35:47.930 --> 00:35:49.580
to the conspiracy that
the Republican governor

00:35:49.580 --> 00:35:51.530
threw the election for the Democrats.

00:35:51.530 --> 00:35:53.410
Everything that she said
was complete fiction,

00:35:53.410 --> 00:35:55.510
and not really even believable fiction.

00:35:55.510 --> 00:35:58.070
And since no reasonable
person believes Sidney Powell,

00:35:58.070 --> 00:35:59.090
she really could have benefited

00:35:59.090 --> 00:36:01.010
from Sabaa Tahir's Skillshare class,

00:36:01.010 --> 00:36:02.280
Writing Authentic Fiction:

00:36:02.280 --> 00:36:04.340
How to Build a Believable Character.

00:36:04.340 --> 00:36:05.740
Now that Powell has been fired,

00:36:05.740 --> 00:36:07.967
she'll have lots of time to
focus on that fiction novel,

00:36:07.967 --> 00:36:09.540
"The Scarlet Barrister."

00:36:09.540 --> 00:36:11.130
But Sabaa Tahir's Skillshare class

00:36:11.130 --> 00:36:12.670
walks you through her personal process

00:36:12.670 --> 00:36:15.690
for crafting authentic
and believable characters.

00:36:15.690 --> 00:36:17.370
It's packed with exercises, prompts,

00:36:17.370 --> 00:36:18.910
and tried-and-true techniques.

00:36:18.910 --> 00:36:20.200
This class will teach you how to create

00:36:20.200 --> 00:36:21.960
real and nuanced characters that feel like

00:36:21.960 --> 00:36:23.780
they could walk right off the page

00:36:23.780 --> 00:36:25.840
and not into a conspiracy theory.

00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:27.420
And, of course, the best
place to learn anything

00:36:27.420 --> 00:36:28.520
is on Skillshare.

